#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "1456cdfb-6ae9-5439-8ebe-e5b41687dfa9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5337" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d1bf0d7128d5e1fffab3f4323f4f4c3ef3b7cae0e041a0cb2a510045d570f40.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. The diagram shows that the parabola has a vertex at $(14,2)$ and passes through the point $\\left(12,6\\right).$\n\nThe equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k) = (14,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 = 4p(x-14).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(12,6),$ we substitute $x=12$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6-2)^{2} & =4p(12-14) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-2) \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =-8(x-14).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y= 0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =-8(x-14) \\\\ (0-2)^{2} & =-8(x-14) \\\\ 4 & =-8(x-14) \\\\ x-14 & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}+14 \\\\ x & =\\frac{27}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $\\dfrac{27}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "77303537-c614-5269-a22e-0bd412e7fa99" :question/math-academy-id "e-5339" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola $(y-2)^2=4(x+1)$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the points of intersection of the parabola with the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4(x+1) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =2\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $y$-axis $(0,0)$ and $(0,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "4ce7b936-de41-5aeb-b980-80d9fa91314f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5340" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b08115f7ede4b4caf8704e893ec48ec78d01870ac3df86a691d47f51ba832d8.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. The diagram shows that the parabola has a vertex at $(7,-4)$ and passes through the point $\\left(5,-6\\right).$\n\nThe equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k) = (7,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+4)^2=4p(x-7).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,-6),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+4)^{2} & =4p(5-7) \\\\ 4 & =4p(-2) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+4)^{2}=-2(x-7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x= 0.$ So, we substitute $x= 0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+4)^{2} & =-2(0-7) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =14 \\\\ y+4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{14} \\\\ y & =-4\\pm \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-4-\\sqrt{14}$ and $-4+\\sqrt{14}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a12aea1c-c3b3-575b-9749-72d8629fcb98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094676} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb23db4-408f-5c84-8753-c37ba86e241f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094677} {:answer/id #uuid "c845e4a4-ad56-58de-a58b-fe5cd1d78e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094678} {:answer/id #uuid "15359918-1f3e-5d67-954c-1cfd40ba2d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094679} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce42d7a-d8b5-5cac-ba6d-014c71515c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094680}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e9bd1337-0a5a-55ad-b855-c3e655576ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094675}] :question/id #uuid "d128bb5c-b2a0-55d9-a9bb-7094e5734308" :question/math-academy-id "q-14242" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d457bfe2ac8b0a86da15e2c34704ae5011a58ad8c148905aa1df12a674544f6c.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b18a44d7-d4b0-55cb-86af-0e13704b8df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b18a44d7-d4b0-55cb-86af-0e13704b8df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376985} :answer-field/id #uuid "44eeaa7e-95d0-594d-b1b9-89222d742f44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ed7cc33-cf3b-5b34-a2ab-03c46db15415" :question/math-academy-id "q-251694" :question/problem "![](images/7e/7e3d4078888350baccee76e73ab9b4a80d2ec95359fbe965bef3dd6a07e5c3b5.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-6,4)$ and passes through the point $(-4,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-6,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-4,8),$ we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8-4)^{2} & =4p(-4+6) \\\\ (4)^{2} & =4p\\cdot (2) \\\\ 16 & =8p \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =8(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =8(x+6) \\\\ 16 & =8(x+6) \\\\ 2 & =x+6 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4f84fb-e01c-5de9-8a75-f717912ae5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b4f84fb-e01c-5de9-8a75-f717912ae5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376988} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a3dacb-4323-51ba-b4c3-d89cd23af82b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05525262-11b5-5c22-9660-edbf983c26e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-251697" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd628bef568023d079cff41ed68efae949a0259822d79273671f633036e743ca.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,2)$ and passes through the point $(21,-4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n{(y-2)}^{2}=4p(x-3).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(21,-4),$ we substitute $x=21$ and $y=-4$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-4-2)}^{2} & =4p(21-3) \\\\ {(-6)}^{2} & =4p\\cdot (18) \\\\ 36 & =72p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =2(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-2)^{2} & =2(x-3) \\\\ 4 & =2(x-3) \\\\ 2 & =x-3 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab9359e1-2230-572b-b145-165bd7eeb22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab9359e1-2230-572b-b145-165bd7eeb22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291377} :answer-field/id #uuid "d38bb03c-5f69-54cf-9a38-955ba1cb544f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292378} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f83b49c-5263-5a91-8e87-dd8dccdaf781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f83b49c-5263-5a91-8e87-dd8dccdaf781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291324} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5adab7c-e4c7-5d86-8e1b-e14e115b5f92" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3651a6f9-db8c-55ba-9b38-b5a076f97109" :question/math-academy-id "q-251784" :question/problem "![](images/af/afb7abacdffafd634165fd1f35868878b2bc420df6a173b48f6a17c7638f7dec.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the **exact values** of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-3,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-5),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =4p(-5-4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(-9) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =-(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =-(0-4) \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-5$  and $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf229ea4-be5b-5001-9fc2-49258187ce94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf229ea4-be5b-5001-9fc2-49258187ce94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291453} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7860a9b-7728-5971-a35a-bae3745118ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0acb67a-a7e6-5061-89d3-c3088fc5fb2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-251796" :question/problem "![](images/ce/cec5eacc688d3c3a619aeb6ede2b1c9f9290b8045699259d4c1d933ecefd1835.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-6)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-6)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-4)^{2} & =4p(3+6) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4(y+6) \\\\ 16 & =4(y+6) \\\\ 4 & =y+6 \\\\ y & =4-6 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d76beb-d71a-5dfa-94a8-e101972d6f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\sqrt{6}-1$ and $-\\sqrt{6}+1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339439} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5a1f0b-7284-5bbc-b1e7-2ff8ad7d7d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339524} {:answer/id #uuid "37cbab4f-5a5d-53a0-989a-2bba919f1c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{13}{3}$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339629} {:answer/id #uuid "444fa7d9-40fd-53f5-9b43-5cab17eacd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339731} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a5235b-49d2-541d-b7c7-7db7eaa4f152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5a1f0b-7284-5bbc-b1e7-2ff8ad7d7d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339524} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6157a6-9ea9-521a-b21f-1bebe23c029e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fb00539-5721-5d8a-80d7-0dcf285c8845" :question/math-academy-id "q-73831" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a9d728a5927f380bbc88445c6a409974d94050040f23000cce50f70de436917.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-6,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-3,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,8),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+3)^{2} & =4p(8+1) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =(0+1) \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $-4$ and $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18731f91-5a2b-5b17-a387-eb4cc9a4d188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,-\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339346} {:answer/id #uuid "20d59ae8-b207-56f8-a181-059ef32b89f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no intersection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339410} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e77b72-b0aa-5aa2-80c3-74d69e6d1bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-\\sqrt{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340763} {:answer/id #uuid "e98d2c48-e98a-5bd8-bf7e-745db4c2794a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-2\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341189} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c3edd0-3761-5700-a2bb-55bbe3d60703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,-2\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d59ae8-b207-56f8-a181-059ef32b89f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no intersection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339410} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1b9401a-2720-53ab-b421-d5879f6deca9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6253cbd5-f5b5-5898-ac4f-5142a7aaca73" :question/math-academy-id "q-73847" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}=3(x-2)$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =3(0-2) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the equation ${y}^{2}=-6$ has no solutions, we conclude that there are no intersection points."}]}
